  "title": "Creatures can jump — and the body decides what a jump does",
  "question": "Does the hop read as a hop? And does the flat one read as gliding down while the square one drops?",
  "context_md": "Two creatures, side by side, given the **same push**.\n\n- **left** — five cells wide, two tall\n- **right** — three by three\n\nThey are made of the same stuff and they are **exactly the same weight — nine cells each** — so they leave the ledge at the same speed. Everything that happens after that is the shape catching the air, and nothing in the game knows which creature is which: it measures how wide each one is against how tall.\n\n**A** is the game as it was this morning: they walk off the ledge and sink.\n**B** is with the new jump.\n\nMeasured on a taller ledge than this one (the picture is cropped in so you can see them at all): the flat one stays up **113 frames** to the square one's **69** — same weight, same push, and it comes down at less than half the speed.\n\nWhat I am unsure about is the **feel**: whether the arc reads as a hop rather than as something being flung, and whether the slow one reads as gliding or as floating.\n\nThe ants that come with the game do **not** jump and are untouched by this — falls per move on the foraging scene is unchanged to three decimals.",
